WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.670
I think it's a truism in science that if you can see something,

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:04.780
you can study it.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.590
What's really exciting about GFP is it has allowed us to see so 

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.190
much within the cells that we were not aware of before.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:23.460
When I heard about GFP in that seminar in 1989,

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:26.690
I was very excited about it.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:31.370
I think I was the only person in the audience that was working on a 

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:32.550
transparent animal,

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.090
the nematode Caenorhabditis elegans,

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:40.060
and at the same time interested in knowing where genes were expressed.

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:44.450
And I realized that if we could get the DNA for GFP,

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:48.950
we could insert that into the genes we were interested in.

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:54.950
And now those genes would cause the production of GFP and 

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:59.670
we could see that in the living transparent animal just by shining blue 

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:03.570
light onto the animal and seeing which cells turned green.

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:07.460
So the first experiment was to actually try to see, 

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:13.510
could GFP be made in something other than the jellyfish?

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:15.350
At the time,

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:19.630
most of the people working on GFP, 

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:24.950
they all thought this was going to be impossible.

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:34.950
But the person who actually did the first experiment in the lab was Ghia.

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:36.650
My name is Ghia Euskirchen.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:41.520
I was a rotation student in Marty Chalfie's lab. When Marty called me up and 

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:44.330
presented this fluorescence project,

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:49.080
I was actually really excited because for my master's thesis worked on fluorescence and he 

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:53.550
thought that the project with GFP might be a good match and 

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:54.780
in general,

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:55.790
who doesn't like fluorescence?

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.800
It's really beautiful and it's just fun to work with.

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:01.120
So from the very start,

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:06.790
what I wanted and what Douglas wanted was to be able to 

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:11.060
show that GFP could actually form a fluorescent 

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:12.080
protein,

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:16.180
one that we could shine blue light on and see green 

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:19.990
in other organisms.

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:20.920
Mostly,

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.690
I wasn't really expecting the experiment to work,

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:29.040
one of the big reasons being because none of the other graduate students in the lab really wanted to 

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:29.570
touch it.

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:31.980
They kind of had looks on their faces like,

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:32.680
yeah,

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:33.580
good luck with that.

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:38.730
The problem came in, in that all the people in the field knew that 

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:42.190
the final protein had this arrangement,

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:47.170
this change of the cyclization of the backbone, that 

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:49.980
is the production of that five atom ring.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:58.910
And they were perplexed about this because to see a 

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:03.560
chemical change in a molecule, to them, implied 

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:07.110
that there had to be a converting enzyme.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:10.630
And so they had convinced themselves that that was the case.

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:15.180
And so this was really a fool's errand to just put the 

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:18.710
DNA encoding the GFP into an organism;

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:19.780
it would not work.

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:22.420
We decided to just try this anyway.

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:27.520
I think sometimes it's probably a very good idea to be just willing to try the 

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:32.310
experiments and not be convinced by everyone's reasons of why those 

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:36.250
are sort of silly to do. Being new to molecular biology,

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:41.480
I thought this will give me a chance to learn molecular biology so I was willing to try it.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:42.380
And plus,

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:47.340
I could bring some fluorescence experience to the problem and hopefully 

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:48.890
have something to contribute that way.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:52.500
So the first experiment was to actually try to see, 

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:57.790
could GFP be made in something other than the 

00:03:57.790 --> 00:03:58.580
jellyfish?

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:03.749
And by far the easiest way of looking at this would be to put it into bacteria.

00:04:04.269 --> 00:04:09.079
And so that was the first experiment. When Ghia actually 

00:04:09.079 --> 00:04:10.349
started to do the experiment,

00:04:10.399 --> 00:04:15.109
there were obviously several steps that needed to be done in order for this to work:

00:04:15.319 --> 00:04:17.469
isolate that the original DNA;

00:04:18.819 --> 00:04:22.179
make many copies of it by polymerase chain reaction;

00:04:23.139 --> 00:04:27.689
take that DNA and transform or add that 

00:04:27.699 --> 00:04:32.699
DNA to bacteria so that each bacterium got one copy of 

00:04:32.709 --> 00:04:33.439
the DNA;

00:04:35.119 --> 00:04:40.099
grow up those bacteria and look at it under a microscope that would 

00:04:40.099 --> 00:04:41.749
allow you to see fluorescence.

00:04:42.539 --> 00:04:47.259
The first was to take the DNA that Douglas Prasher had 

00:04:47.259 --> 00:04:51.549
provided us and to make lots of copies of it.

00:04:52.299 --> 00:04:55.549
And I wanted her only to use the coding sequence,

00:04:55.559 --> 00:04:59.659
but the original DNA that Douglas had given us, 

00:05:00.619 --> 00:05:05.269
had not only the coding sequence but some extra 

00:05:05.269 --> 00:05:07.749
jellyfish DNA on either side.

00:05:09.539 --> 00:05:11.489
I just didn't want to have anything extra,

00:05:11.489 --> 00:05:13.039
I just wanted to have what we needed.

00:05:13.349 --> 00:05:18.039
So we used the method of polymerase chain reaction, PCR,

00:05:18.169 --> 00:05:22.879
which starts by having pieces of DNA, primers, that will 

00:05:22.879 --> 00:05:27.639
bind to specific parts of the DNA and allow that 

00:05:27.649 --> 00:05:32.439
part of the DNA to be copied and amplified so that you get many 

00:05:32.449 --> 00:05:32.999
copies of it.

00:05:34.299 --> 00:05:38.819
So we were careful in the sense that we just wanted to use the coding 

00:05:38.819 --> 00:05:43.319
sequence. In 1992 when Ghia did these experiments,

00:05:43.989 --> 00:05:48.379
this copying of the DNA using PCR 

00:05:49.119 --> 00:05:50.749
was prone to errors.

00:05:51.209 --> 00:05:55.599
There were lots of ways that this could go wrong.

00:05:55.939 --> 00:05:59.789
And so not all the DNA was a perfect copy of something.

00:05:59.789 --> 00:06:02.799
And once it wasn't a perfect copy, then when it was copied,

00:06:03.196 --> 00:06:07.056
maybe that same mistake would then be propagated.

00:06:08.046 --> 00:06:09.766
This didn't bother us at all,

00:06:10.216 --> 00:06:14.776
and the reason it didn't bother us is we were going to put all the individual copies 

00:06:15.126 --> 00:06:16.756
into different bacteria.

00:06:18.046 --> 00:06:19.866
So if a bad copy got in,

00:06:20.746 --> 00:06:22.466
maybe the protein wouldn't be made,

00:06:22.816 --> 00:06:26.106
but enough of the bacteria would have good copies,

00:06:26.546 --> 00:06:29.456
and if the product was going to be fluorescent,

00:06:30.026 --> 00:06:31.956
those bacteria should be fluorescent.

00:06:32.606 --> 00:06:37.006
The next part was inducing the E. coli to express the protein 

00:06:37.276 --> 00:06:38.806
and had two samples.

00:06:38.966 --> 00:06:41.696
One were E. coli with the plasmid,

00:06:41.976 --> 00:06:43.266
also known as a vector,

00:06:43.326 --> 00:06:48.266
which had the GFP insert, and then another tube of E. coli that only 

00:06:48.396 --> 00:06:49.526
had plasmid,

00:06:49.536 --> 00:06:50.886
no GFP insert.

00:06:51.666 --> 00:06:56.256
But it was a little fuzzy because no one had really done this, had really looked at GFP outside 

00:06:56.846 --> 00:06:59.306
its native jellyfish.

00:06:59.436 --> 00:07:03.726
It was important to have that control of E. coli that weren't expressing 

00:07:03.726 --> 00:07:08.616
GFP and so you could see that there was a difference in fluorescence between the 

00:07:08.616 --> 00:07:13.346
two. I was fairly stunned at the time because the whole experiment wasn't supposed to be that easy.

00:07:13.636 --> 00:07:18.626
So it was really surprising it actually worked. And so 

00:07:18.626 --> 00:07:21.636
that first day in October,

00:07:21.636 --> 00:07:24.086
about a month after she started graduate school,

00:07:24.606 --> 00:07:29.596
Ghia was able to look down the microscope and actually see bacteria that were 

00:07:29.596 --> 00:07:33.006
glowing green when they were stimulated by blue light.

00:07:34.156 --> 00:07:38.716
I found out several years later that at least three other groups 

00:07:39.516 --> 00:07:41.456
tried to do the same experiment.

00:07:42.286 --> 00:07:46.846
This shows you that no one has a lock on any one idea.

00:07:46.856 --> 00:07:49.876
Lots of people come up with a lot of interesting ideas.

00:07:50.646 --> 00:07:55.496
But all these three other groups did something different from what we 

00:07:55.496 --> 00:07:55.836
did.

00:07:56.646 --> 00:08:00.656
What they found is they needed to cut that DNA 

00:08:02.036 --> 00:08:03.126
to be able to add it.

00:08:03.438 --> 00:08:08.428
And so they used enzymes, called restriction enzymes, to cut at very 

00:08:08.428 --> 00:08:09.688
specific places.

00:08:10.878 --> 00:08:11.028
Now,

00:08:11.028 --> 00:08:14.188
the enzyme that they used 

00:08:15.928 --> 00:08:17.818
did include the coding sequence,

00:08:18.278 --> 00:08:20.688
but it also included extra DNA,

00:08:21.818 --> 00:08:23.768
and no one actually knows why,

00:08:24.458 --> 00:08:28.088
but those extra pieces are a disaster.

00:08:28.628 --> 00:08:33.618
Having those extra bits of DNA prevent the production of the 

00:08:33.618 --> 00:08:34.788
fluorescent product.

00:08:35.878 --> 00:08:39.508
So we were very fortunate because we didn't have the extra bits.

00:08:40.078 --> 00:08:44.528
Our experiment worked the first time, we had green fluorescent 

00:08:44.528 --> 00:08:45.188
bacteria.

00:08:46.048 --> 00:08:50.538
They put their carefully produced 

00:08:50.548 --> 00:08:52.708
DNA into organisms,

00:08:53.088 --> 00:08:54.188
it didn't work.

00:08:55.088 --> 00:08:57.658
And you'll remember that if it doesn't work,

00:08:57.668 --> 00:09:00.788
maybe that's not because they've made a mistake,

00:09:01.538 --> 00:09:06.148
but maybe because there's that elusive converting 

00:09:06.148 --> 00:09:09.888
enzyme that's needed to make the final fluorescent product.

00:09:11.168 --> 00:09:15.358
Their conclusion might have been, probably was, oh,

00:09:16.078 --> 00:09:17.988
GFP would have been a great thing,

00:09:18.238 --> 00:09:20.588
but it needs something else for it to work.

00:09:21.458 --> 00:09:22.728
Our conclusion was,

00:09:22.738 --> 00:09:24.128
it doesn't need anything else,

00:09:24.188 --> 00:09:25.918
it works completely on its own,

00:09:26.138 --> 00:09:31.138
it's going to be a very useful way of marking cells and proteins.

00:09:32.278 --> 00:09:37.008
What Ghia's experiment told us was that the idea 

00:09:37.188 --> 00:09:42.028
of having GFP as a marker worked and that gave us hope that we 

00:09:42.028 --> 00:09:44.048
could now put it into other organisms,

00:09:44.418 --> 00:09:48.598
particularly into the nematodes Caenorhabditis elegans,

00:09:49.748 --> 00:09:52.988
the animal that I was working with, so we could start to look at cells. 

00:09:57.478 --> 00:10:02.348
One of the most interesting aspects, more or less sociologically than anything else, 

00:10:02.378 --> 00:10:06.983
for me has been to see how this rather simple 

00:10:06.983 --> 00:10:11.813
experiment of putting GFP into bacteria 

00:10:11.813 --> 00:10:16.083
and worms has expanded as everyone else has added 

00:10:17.023 --> 00:10:20.823
their thoughts and ideas to using this protein.

00:10:21.923 --> 00:10:26.613
It was estimated in 2014, by the people at Chemistry 

00:10:26.613 --> 00:10:31.453
Abstracts, that in the twenty years since our paper was published, that there 

00:10:31.453 --> 00:10:36.293
have been on the order of 160,000 scientific papers using 

00:10:36.293 --> 00:10:37.433
fluorescent proteins.

00:10:38.363 --> 00:10:43.023
And the sheer range of the things that people have 

00:10:43.023 --> 00:10:46.383
tried, thought of, has been really remarkable.

00:10:47.273 --> 00:10:51.713
That first day when I was listening to the seminar 

00:10:52.353 --> 00:10:57.323
where I first learned about GFP, I thought about some experiments that I would like to do,

00:10:58.273 --> 00:11:03.193
but my ideas have been dwarfed by all the really incredible ideas that people 

00:11:03.193 --> 00:11:05.293
have had coming out of this.

00:11:11.263 --> 00:11:12.933
Sometimes people ask me,

00:11:13.213 --> 00:11:17.323
did you think this was the type of experiment that would win a Nobel Prize?

00:11:17.943 --> 00:11:21.633
And I can honestly say that I wasn't thinking about prizes or anything,

00:11:21.633 --> 00:11:24.183
I was more excited about the experiment.

00:11:24.673 --> 00:11:26.373
One might ask the question,

00:11:26.383 --> 00:11:30.433
why would someone interested in developmental biology, in 

00:11:30.443 --> 00:11:31.403
genetics,

00:11:31.483 --> 00:11:36.253
how did I wind up being the person that introduced this 

00:11:36.253 --> 00:11:38.763
very useful tool into biology?

00:11:39.573 --> 00:11:44.533
And I don't have a very good answer to that, other than the fact I was just excited when 

00:11:44.533 --> 00:11:49.513
I heard that first seminar about GFP and realized it would be a very,

00:11:49.523 --> 00:11:51.103
very useful tool.

00:11:51.613 --> 00:11:54.883
And I thought it would be a lot of fun if we did those experiments.

00:11:55.333 --> 00:12:00.083
I think that's actually a very important part of science, is that we're always 

00:12:00.083 --> 00:12:02.523
working on the edge of what we know.

00:12:03.363 --> 00:12:06.043
We're not trying to duplicate things that we already know,

00:12:06.053 --> 00:12:08.410
that doesn't give us more information.

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:11.140
We're trying to understand something new.

00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:15.390
We're not all that great at understanding things that are new.

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:18.740
So it's not maybe a surprise that experiments don't work,

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:21.610
but we keep trying to go after it,

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:25.340
and once we do have those discoveries, or those breakthroughs,

00:12:25.830 --> 00:12:27.460
it's exceptionally rewarding.

